# THE AGE-17 DISCRIMINATING FIXTURE: ONE ATTEMPT, NOT CONSTRUCTED, and what was measured instead.
#
# WHY THIS LOG EXISTS. CR-31-L-2 and CM208-Medium-1 asked for a fixture that discriminates the interrupt-entry
# drain's off-by-one: a run in which an expectation is stamped on the finish request's OWN record. The corrected
# drain (bound PLUS ONE) reaches age bound+1 on its last record and the in-run rule fires; the pre-correction drain
# (exactly the bound) stops one record earlier, leaves age == bound, and reports the expectation open with no error.
# Landing 32 answered the row from the comparison operators and said plainly that no such fixture was built.
# CM209-Info-1 asked for one attempt or a statement that it is owed. This is the attempt.
#
# WHAT IS CLAIMED. The fixture is NOT CONSTRUCTED. No run here produced an expectation stamped on the finish
# request's own record, and no run separates the two drains by a bound failure. Two things ARE measured and are
# claimed as measurements: (a) the rule's stamp source and the counter the drain measures are the same number at
# every one of 251 expectation creations in one run, so the correction's plus-one is exactly one record in the
# rule's own units and not one record of a skewed counter; (b) the reason the boundary stayed empty, which is that
# raise events near a run's end are tens of cycles apart while a record retires about every 5.3 cycles, so the
# finish request's own record usually falls in a gap between raises.
#
# WHAT IS NOT CLAIMED. Not that the age-exactly-bound case is unreachable, and not that this sample is large. Only
# the fault-C sweep frees the raise phase from the finish request, and its onset values collapse to far fewer
# distinct outcomes than onsets, so the free-phase sample is small and zero hits over it bounds nothing. The case is
# reachable by construction, which section 7 names and does not build. The off-by-one fix therefore still rests on
# the comparison operators, with the landing-32 seed-7 run as its non-discriminating check.
#
# PROVENANCE OF THIS RECORD, stated because it matters. The attempt was run by the earlier instance of this role;
# this instance added nine runs (the five single-record thresholds across the crossing and four seeds at one
# threshold, the run names carrying an x) and AUTHORED THIS LOG. Every figure below was re-derived by this instance
# from the run artefacts with the commands shown, not copied from the earlier draft. The earlier draft's own
# summary sentence gave 85 runs and 79 counted survivors; those two counts DO NOT REPRODUCE and are superseded by
# the measured values printed below. Every other figure of that draft reproduced exactly, including the 43 onsets
# collapsing to 22 distinct outcomes, fault B's ceiling of 14, the offset-zero result and the raise spacing.
#
# THE BUILDS. Every build is a SCRATCH build of a mutated tree that was never committed, so no gate identity (the
# flow's filelist_digest over gen_rtl.f plus gen_tb.f) applies to any of them and none is claimed. Each figure is
# the build's own local runner digest, printed by its compile log as "sources sha256" over dv/auto_dv env, tb, isa
# and gen_tb. Every root is a detached "git archive HEAD" of the committed tree with the fault applied on top.
#   Fault A = MUT-NT2 (irq_external withheld once 3572 records have retired), base a68c021:
#       committed drain (bound plus one)            5137b1536b415deb   table name "new"
#       pre-correction drain (exactly the bound)    67dd4d0f3211a706   table name "old"
#   Fault B = MUT-BND1 (every maskable line withheld from the finish request onward), base 2410402:
#       committed drain                             f54b78ebcae677e2   table name "bnew"
#       pre-correction drain + survivor-age probe   7d01913742957474   table name "boldi"
#   Fault C = MUT-BND2 (the same withholding, onset read from +mutbnd_at), base 2410402:
#       committed drain                             9f42cb9ff0d19851   table name "dnew"
#       pre-correction drain                        1339052279e22532   table name "dold"
#       pre-correction drain + survivor-age probe   3711a763aa7722b1   table name "doldi"
#       pre-correction drain + creation probe       98a7df6c0fceb8da   table name "doldp"
#   THE TWO PROBE BUILDS ARE MEASUREMENT AIDS, NOT EVIDENCE ABOUT THE FIX: they add one uvm_info to the checker and
#   nothing else, and any claim about the drain itself is read from an unprobed build.
#   All runs use the irqp program, the storm regime with the multi line mix, and +gen_chk_irq_pending=0 so the entry
#   rule is the only judge: withholding a raised line also makes the pending checker mismatch, and its errors would
#   drown the one count under test. Every other checker is on.
#   TWO LOOP FACTS WORTH KEEPING. A mutant plusarg must not begin with gen_, because the TB's own guard fatals on
#   unknown gen_ names against the knob table (gen_env_pkg.sv); one build was discarded to that and none of its runs
#   appears here. And the interrupt checker's summary prints "bound failures" twice, once plain and once prefixed
#   nmi_internal, so the summariser takes the first match only.

== 0. THE RUN SET, re-derived ==
$ python3 age17_summarise.py <the nine build directories> | wc -l
   rows: 195
$ python3 (histogram over those rows)
   total runs: 195   of which added by this instance: 9
   per build: bnew=42  boldi=13  doldi=52  doldp=1  new=57  old=30
   runs reporting at least one survivor: 48   survivor entries: 90
   survivor age histogram (age:count): 1:12 2:4 3:4 4:3 5:4 6:1 7:12 8:3 9:6 10:2 11:4 12:5 13:13 14:11 15:6
   min age 1   MAX AGE 15   entries at 16 or 17: 0
   fault C onset values: 47   DISTINCT OUTCOMES: 25
   fault B runs: 55   survivor entries: 28   max age: 14  (its onset IS the finish request, so its ceiling sits one record lower)
   READING: the max age over every survivor of every run is 15 and no entry reaches 16 or 17, which is the
   boundary the fixture needs. The onset count against the distinct-outcome count is the honest sample size:
   an onset that falls in a gap between raise events changes nothing, so many onsets give one outcome.

== 1. THE FINISH POINT IS NOT A LEVER: +gen_ut_boot_retire does not move the finish request ==
   Same build (fault A, committed drain), same seed, two boot-retire values. The program ends on its own
   tohost write, so the plusarg is an upper bound this program never reaches. The fixture's original plan,
   steering the finish point with this plusarg, cannot work.
   new    k2490          seed   3 onset          - boot_retire 2490 FAIL finish  3710 drained 18 records  3728 boundfail   1 open  0 ages [-] fired_at [3718]
   new    k2500          seed   3 onset          - boot_retire 2500 FAIL finish  3710 drained 18 records  3728 boundfail   1 open  0 ages [-] fired_at [3718]

== 2. THE OFFSET-ZERO MEASUREMENT: the rule's stamp source and the drain's counter are one number ==
   The rule ages an expectation as st.order minus order_at, with order_at the last record the checker has
   processed (gen_checkers_pkg.sv). The drain measures vif.evt_retired_count, the bridge counter
   (gen_env_pkg.sv). If those differed at a raise, the corrected drain's extra record would not be one record
   in the rule's own units. The creation probe prints both at every creation:
$ command grep -ac "PROBE create" doldp/s3t3596/stdout.log
   251
$ command grep -ao "PROBE create .*offset -\?[0-9]*" ... | grep -o "offset -\?[0-9]*$" | sort | uniq -c
       251 offset 0
   So the two are the same number at every creation in this run, and the plus-one is exactly one record of
   the rule's own clock. This closes the one way the correction's size could have been wrong.

== 3. WHY THE BOUNDARY STAYED EMPTY, measured on fault C, seed 3, onset 3596 ==
$ command grep -ao "finish_req seen at cycle [0-9]*, commands consumed [0-9]*, retired [0-9]*" ... | head -1
   finish_req seen at cycle 22472, commands consumed 65, retired 3686
$ command grep -ao "PROBE create .*raise cycle .*offset -\?[0-9]*" ... | tail -6
   PROBE create lines 24140 raise cycle 22189: stamp last_st.order 3629, bridge evt_retired_count 3629, offset 0
   PROBE create lines 20200 raise cycle 22416: stamp last_st.order 3674, bridge evt_retired_count 3674, offset 0
   PROBE create lines 02008 raise cycle 22442: stamp last_st.order 3679, bridge evt_retired_count 3679, offset 0
   PROBE create lines 00012 raise cycle 22486: stamp last_st.order 3689, bridge evt_retired_count 3689, offset 0
   PROBE create lines 00940 raise cycle 22491: stamp last_st.order 3690, bridge evt_retired_count 3690, offset 0
   PROBE create lines 20006 raise cycle 22574: stamp last_st.order 3702, bridge evt_retired_count 3702, offset 0
$ python3 (the gaps and the record rate from those six lines)
   raise records: 3629, 3674, 3679, 3689, 3690, 3702
   raise cycles:  22189, 22416, 22442, 22486, 22491, 22574
   gaps in cycles between consecutive raises: 227, 26, 44, 5, 83
   cycles per record over that span: (22574-22189)/(3702-3629) = 5.27
   the finish request retired on record 3686; the raises either side are on 3679 and 3689, a miss by 3
   The survivors of that run, from the probe's own lines:
   PROBE survivor lines 00012 nmi 0 order_at 3689 last order 3703 age 14
   PROBE survivor lines 00940 nmi 0 order_at 3690 last order 3703 age 13
   PROBE survivor lines 20006 nmi 0 order_at 3702 last order 3703 age 1
   Their stamps are 3689, 3690 and 3702 against the pre-correction drain's last record, giving 14, 13 and 1.
   An age of 17 needs a stamp on record 3686 itself. Nothing was stamped there, so no expectation could reach
   the bound. A raise falls inside any one named record only a fraction of the time, which is why a seed or
   onset sweep is the wrong instrument for this boundary.

== 4. WHAT THE TWO DRAINS DID, side by side ==
   Fault A ran on both drains. No seed differs in its bound-failure count. Three seeds differ in the OPPOSITE
   direction: the committed drain's extra record admits one more late raise, so it reports one MORE survivor,
   which is not the boundary. Fault B's onset IS the finish request, so it cannot reach the boundary at all.
$ python3 (pair the fault-A rows by seed and compare bound failures)
   seeds on the committed drain: 55   also on the pre-correction drain: 30
   seeds whose BOUND-FAILURE count differs between the drains: 0  []
   seeds whose OPEN count differs: 3  ['13', '18', '26']
     seed 13: committed bf=1 open=1   pre-correction bf=1 open=0
     seed 18: committed bf=0 open=1   pre-correction bf=0 open=0
     seed 26: committed bf=0 open=1   pre-correction bf=0 open=0
   READING: zero seeds differ in the bound-failure count, which is the signal the fixture needs. The open-count
   differences run the other way and are the extra record admitting a later raise, not the boundary.

== 5. WHAT IS OWED, AND THE CONSTRUCTION A LATER ATTEMPT SHOULD BUILD ==
   The boundary needs a raise event inside the finish request's own record, which no sweep controls. A
   stimulus-side trigger does control it: the interrupt driver already arms a timed action on an observed
   event, the mechanism the timed-NMI knob uses. A driver hook that raises one maskable line on the finish
   request itself, on a line the DUT cannot take because it is withheld, places the stamp on the finish record
   BY CONSTRUCTION rather than by luck. That is TB work with its own trust triad, not a mutant, and it is NOT
   BUILT here. Until it exists the off-by-one correction rests on the operators, and the retirement-stall
   residual stays open behind it.

